// `ctxc test PATH [options] [FILTER...] -- ARGS...` gives ARGS to every test, which reads
// them with test::args. Here a test takes a game's directory, and is skipped without it.
//# write game/chitin.key:
//| KEY V1  and the rest of it

// Without arguments: the test that needs the install says so, and the other runs.
//# $ ctxc test . install
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| ./t.ctx:4: skip: the_install_has_its_key_file: no game install: give its directory after `--`
//| tests: 2 run, 1 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped, 2 not selected

// With the directory: it runs. Filters and options come before the `--`.
//# $ ctxc test . -v install -- {dir}/game
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| ./t.ctx:4: pass: the_install_has_its_key_file
//| ./t.ctx:17: pass: needs_no_install
//| tests: 2 run, 2 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 2 not selected

// A directory without the game: the test fails with the error it returned.
//# $ ctxc test . key_file -- {dir}/nowhere
//# exit: 1
//# stdout:
//| ./t.ctx:4: fail: the_install_has_its_key_file: error: fs::not_found
//| tests: 1 run, 0 passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected

// Everything after the first `--` is the tests', whatever it looks like: an option, a
// test's name, an empty word, another `--`. Each test is given all of it.
//# $ ctxc test . shows -- --list install "" -- "two words"
//# exit: 1
//# stdout:
//| argument 0: `--list`
//| argument 1: `install`
//| argument 2: ``
//| argument 3: `--`
//| argument 4: `two words`
//| ./t.ctx:22: fail: shows_its_arguments: test::args{ &t }.len == 0: 5 == 0
//| tests: 1 run, 0 passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected

// A `--` with nothing after it, and no `--`: no arguments.
//# $ ctxc test . shows --
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| tests: 1 run, 1 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected
//# $ ctxc test . shows
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| tests: 1 run, 1 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected

// An option after the `--` is not the runner's, and one it doesn't know before it is.
//# $ ctxc test . --list shows -- --nope
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| ./t.ctx:20: shows_its_arguments
//# $ ctxc test . --nope -- x
//# exit: 2
//# stderr:
//| usage: ctxc test PATH [--list] [-v] [--no-skips] [--jobs N] [--timeout SECONDS] [FILTER...] [-- ARG...]: runs the tests whose names contain a FILTER, or all of them, and gives them the ARGs

// The environment is another way in, which needs nothing of the runner: a test that takes
// a Proc reads it.
//# $ ctxc test . environment
//# env: GAME_DIR=elsewhere
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| tests: 1 run, 1 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected
//# $ ctxc test . environment
//# exit: 1
//# stdout:
//| ./t.ctx:28: fail: reads_the_environment: proc::env{ &proc, name = "GAME_DIR" } == "elsewhere": null == elsewhere
//| tests: 1 run, 0 passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped, 3 not selected
